            {
              "id": "2c7a64a3-67e3-3964-a7be-c37bf4d3b030",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure. This can lead to predictable randomness which can be exploited by an attacker. The `std::rand()` function in C++ is a non-cryptographic random number generator and is not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `std::rand()` is used to generate a random number:\n\n```cpp\nA[i] = ADataType(float(std::rand()) / float(RAND_MAX) - 0.5) * 100\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, you can use the `std::random_device` class from the `<random>` library, which is intended to produce random integers with the highest quality of randomness provided by the implementation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(-50, 50);\n\nA[i] = ADataType(dis(gen));\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is used to seed the Mersenne Twister engine (`std::mt19937`). `std::uniform_real_distribution` is then used to generate a random float between -50 and 50.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<random>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "2c7a64a3-67e3-3964-a7be-c37bf4d3b030"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dc3f63b1-c9bf-3cd6-bc07-12595fcb2e18",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure. This can lead to predictable randomness which can be exploited by an attacker. The `std::rand()` function in C++ is a non-cryptographic random number generator and is not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `std::rand()` is used to generate a random number:\n\n```cpp\nA[i] = ADataType(float(std::rand()) / float(RAND_MAX) - 0.5) * 100\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, you can use the `std::random_device` class from the `<random>` library, which is intended to produce random integers with the highest quality of randomness provided by the implementation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can fix the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(-50, 50);\n\nA[i] = ADataType(dis(gen));\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is used to seed the Mersenne Twister engine (`std::mt19937`). `std::uniform_real_distribution` is then used to generate a random float between -50 and 50.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<random>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "dc3f63b1-c9bf-3cd6-bc07-12595fcb2e18"
                ]
              }
